1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:17,800
This module is about what makes an app feel like it belongs on a phone, rather than like a
website in a frame. It knows where it is. It can reach you later. It moves with intention.

2
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:23,800
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:40,600
First, a distinction that saves a lot of confusion. MapKit and Core Location are two
different things. MapKit is the map — what you see, the pins, the callouts. Core Location is
the hardware underneath: GPS, heading, geofencing. MapKit uses Core Location.

4
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:56,600
A map needs three things: a coordinate to centre on, a region defining how far out you're
zoomed, and annotations for your pins. Add a delegate and you can draw custom pins and
decide what happens when one is tapped.

5
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:22,200
One decision. There's a Google Maps SDK for iOS with richer place data — phone numbers,
opening hours. But MapKit is native, needs no API key, no billing account and no credit
card, and is free for app development. Google's requires a billing account before it will

6
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:32,200
Now permissions, and this is where people lose hours. Anything touching location, camera or
photos needs two things — and everybody remembers the first one.

7
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:50,200
You request permission in code. But you must also declare it in a file called Info.plist.
Skip that and the feature doesn't work — it doesn't error clearly, it simply doesn't happen.
And App Store review rejects builds that use a protected capability without one.

8
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:59,000
The keys must be exact. For location while using the app it's
NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription. There are equivalents for camera, photo library and
microphone.

9
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:15,800
And whatever text you write is shown to the user word for word in the permission alert. So
write a real reason — 'used to show cinemas near you', not just the word 'location'. That
sentence is part of your user experience.

10
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,200
Next, notifications. There are two kinds and the difference is architectural.

11
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:27,800
Push notifications, through Apple's APNs service, are sent from a server. Local
notifications are scheduled on the device itself.

12
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:51,800
The reference app uses local notifications, and the reasoning is practical. APNs needs a
backend to send from, plus a paid Apple Developer account at ninety-nine dollars a year,
plus certificates that expire and need rotating. This app has no backend, so local

13
00:02:51,800 --> 00:03:09,000
But be honest about the limitation, because it's real. A local notification can only fire on
a schedule the device already knows about. If a screening were cancelled, the device would
have no way to find out. That requires push and a server.

14
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:25,800
Two details in this implementation worth copying. It checks a user setting first, so if
reminders are off nothing is scheduled. And cancelling a booking also cancels its pending
reminder — so you're never reminded about a ticket you no longer hold.

15
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:45,000
Briefly, two more capabilities. For photos there are two APIs and people mix them up.
PHPicker is the modern one and what you should use for choosing an existing photo — it runs
outside your app and doesn't even need photo-library permission for pictures the user

16
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:58,600
The older UIImagePickerController has been soft-deprecated since iOS fourteen. But here's
the catch: PHPicker cannot take a photo. For camera capture, the older API is still what you
need. Most real apps use both.

17
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:19,800
And animation. UIKit lets you animate a change over a duration, and the duration is the
whole point. Set it to zero and the change snaps. Give it three tenths of a second and it
feels intentional. That's much of the difference between an app that feels cheap and one

18
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:37,800
Here's it all together. Real cinema pins from real coordinates. Tapping one opens a callout
and routes into the cinema's details and showtimes. And a reminder firing as a banner and on
the lock screen, carrying the film, the venue, the seats and the time.

19
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:40,300
Let's recap the key points.

20
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:42,800
Here is your practice exercise.

21
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:48,800
That completes the technical curriculum. Chapter eight assembles all seven modules into one
complete application.
